DIFFERENT TYPES OF TOLERANCE USED IN MECHANICAL DRAWING

Fundamentals
Tolerance is the allowable variation from a specified dimension so parts can be manufactured, assembled, and function reliably.
Nominal size is the target or intended dimension.
Upper and lower limits define the acceptable range.
Tighter tolerances increase precision and cost; looser ones improve manufacturability.

1. LIMIT TOLERANCES
Specifies the absolute maximum and minimum permissible sizes of a feature.
No plus/minus symbol is used; both limits are written directly.
Example:
  19.98 mm  MIN SIZE
  20.02 mm  MAX SIZE
Acceptable range: 19.98 mm to 20.02 mm

2. UNILATERAL TOLERANCES
Allows variation in only one direction from the nominal size (all above or all below).
(a) Positive unilateral
  20.00 +0.05
        0
  Range: 20.00 mm to 20.05 mm
(b) Negative unilateral
  20.00  0
        -0.05
  Range: 19.95 mm to 20.00 mm

3. BILATERAL TOLERANCES
Allows variation in both the positive and negative directions from the nominal size.
The two deviations may be equal or unequal.
Example (equal):
  20.00 ± 0.05 mm
  Range: 19.95 mm to 20.05 mm
Nominal size = 20.00 mm

Quick reference
Limit     : max size / min size written as two values
Unilateral: deviation on one side only (+/0 or 0/-)
Bilateral : deviation on both sides (+ and -)

